  "raw_text": "58\n\nTaih dakbaia\n \n(v-trans; intrans:\n \nTaih dakwaia\n)\n \n–\n \nto break off, to cut off\n \n/ romper\n\nTiala dakbaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto wean\n \n/ destetar\n\nDakni\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \ngroup, flock, gang, crowd, congregation, multitude\n \n/ grupo, rebaño, pandilla, multitud, congregación\n\nAiklabaia dakni\n \n(\nmy52,72\n) (n/s)\n \n–\n \narmy, troops, battalion\n \n/ ejército, tropas, batallón\n\nAiwawanra daknika\n \n(n-agent/s-agente)\n \n–\n \nchorus of singers, group of singers, choir\n \n/ coro de cantantes, grupo de\ncantantes, coro\n\nDakni tara pura kakai\nr\na\n \n(\njl17\n-\nKangrigisan Daknika Pura Kakairika\n)\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \ndistrict overseer\n \n/\n\nsuperintendente de distrito\n \n(\nSee also/\n \nV\ner también:\n \nImpaki pura kakaira\n \n–\n \ntravelling overseer\n, circuit\n\noverseer\n \n/ superintendente viajante, superintendente de circuito\n)\n\nAlmuk nani daknika\n \n–\n \nbody of elders\n \n/ cuerpo de ancianos\n\nAlmuk nani\n \ndaknika t\nâ\n \nbrabrira\n \n(a/t:\n \nKangrigisan almukka nani daknika pura kakaira\n)\n \n–\n \nCoordinator of\n\nthe body of elders\n \n/\n \nCoordinador\n \ndel\n \ncuerpo\n \nde\n \nancianos\n\nDakni wiria\n \n(\nlrw-Luk 12:32\n)\n \n–\n \nlittle flock\n \n/ pequeño rebaño\n\nLâ bapaia daknika\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \njudicial committee\n \n/ comité judicial\n\nTâ Bri Daknika\n \n–\n \nGoverning Body\n \n/\n \nCuerpo Gobernante\n\n(Ex/Ej(\nmwb-12/18\n):\n \nTâ bri daknika lukanka ba kangrigisan nani ra spirit lâka ra karna daukan.\n \n–\n \nThe governing body’s\n\ndecision made the congregations strong in a spiritual way.\n \n/ La decisión del cuerpo gobernante fortaleció a las\ncongregaciones de una manera espiritual.)\n\nPas handat mani tâ bri daknika\n \n–\n \nfirst-century governing body\n \n/ cuerpo gobernante del siglo primero\n\nTâ brabrira nani daknika hilp mamunra ba\n \n–\n \nhelper to the Governing Body\n \n/ ayudante del Cuerpo\nGobernante\n\nDaktar\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \ndoctor\n \n/ doctor\n\nDakwaia\n \n(v-intrans; trans:\n \nDakbaia\n)\n \n–\n \nto separate, to break apart, to be separated, to be broken apart, to be taken away\n\n/ separarse, ser quitado\n\nAihka aihka dakwaia\n \n(v-intrans; trans:\n \nAihka aihka dakbaia\n)\n \n–\n \nto separate, to disperse\n \n/ apartar, separar,\ndispersar\n\n(Ex/Ej\n(\ndb-Blasi Sturka 10:18\n):\n \nWitin nani turwi aihka aihka dakwi taki iwan\n \n–\n \nThey dispersed and came to live separately\n \n/\nEllos se dispersaron y vinieron a vivir por separado)\n\nDakwi takaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto separate, to become separated\n \n(either marital, or from a friend, or from a church or other\norganization) / separarse (ya sea conyugal, o de un amigo, o de una iglesia u otra organización)\n\nDakwi takaia lâka\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nseparation\n \n(either marital, or from a friend, or from a church or other\norganization) / separación (ya sea marital, o de un amigo, o de una iglesia u otra organización)\n\nSilp dakwi takaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto disassociate oneself\n \n/ desasociarse\n\nTaih dakwaia\n \n(v-intrans; trans:\n \nTaih dakbaia\n)\n \n–\n \nto break off, to cut off\n \n/ romper\n\nDala\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \ndollar, cordoba\n \n/ dólar, c\nó\nrdoba\n\n(\nEx/Ej:\n \ndala w\nâ\nl\n \n–\n \n2 dollars, 2\n \ncordobas\n \n/ 2 dólares, 2 córdobas\n)\n\nDama\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \ngrandfather, ancestor, forefather, old man\n \n/ abuelo, antepasado, anciano\n \n(See also/ Ver también:\n \nKuka\n\n–\n \ngrandmother\n \n/ abuela)\n\n(Construct:\n \nDamiki\n \n–\n \nmy grandfather\n... / mi abuelo\n…\n;\n \nDamikam\n \n–\n \nyour grandfather\n... / tu abuelo\n…\n;\n \nAi damika\n \n–\n \nhis/her/their\ngrandfather\n... / su abuelo;\n \nDamia\n \n–\n \nconstruct of:\n \nold man\n \n/ forma constriva de: anciano)\n\n(Ex/Ej(\nlfb33\n):\n \nMani ailal ningkara, Obed king Debit damika kan.\n \n–\n \nMany years after, Obed was King\n \nDavid’s grandfather.\n \n/ Muchos\naños después, Obed era el abuelo del rey David.)\n\nDamika aisika\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \ngreat grandfather\n \n/ bisabuelo\n\nDamika nani\n \n–\n \ngrandparents\n \n/ abuelos\n\nDamni\n \n(adj)\n \n–\n \nsweet, flavorful\n \n/ dulce, gustoso, sabroso\n\n(Ex/Ej(\nfy97\n):\n \ndiara damni kum kum piaia\n \n–\n \nsome sweet things to eat\n \n/ algunas cosas dulces para comer)\n\nDamni daukaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto sweeten\n \n/ endulzar\n\nDamni takaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto become sweet\n \n/ volverse dulce\n\nDamni taya\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \ncandy wrapper\n \n/ envoltura de dulces\n\nDamnika\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nsweetness, flavor\n \n/ dulzura, gusto, sabor\n\n(Note/Nota:\n \ndba,dbta,db-Matiu 5:13,dictu\n \nuse this word to say\n \nsaltiness; lrw\n \nuses\n \nDamnira\n \n/\n \ndba,dbta,db-Matiu 5:13,dictu\n\nusa esta palabra para decir salinidad;\n \nlrw\n \nusa\n \nDamnira\n)\n\nKwah damni dusa\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nfig tree\n \n/ higuera",
